\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models initialized from vision-language models (VLMs) generalize well semantically, but VLM pre-training emphasizes high-level understanding and neglects low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-based policies show the opposite profile: future-frame pre-training grounds spatial detail yet lacks semantic robustness. We present UP-VLA, a single autoregressive model that trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) on image-text data and future visual prediction on robot video, and then conditions action generation on both a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens, using unified prompts and attention masks. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08 versus 3.06 for GR-1 (about 33\% higher), and on ABCD$\rightarrow$D it reaches 4.42 versus 4.21. On a real Franka Panda it improves unseen-object grasping and precise manipulation, and ablations attribute the semantic gains to MMU and the spatial gains to prediction.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large VLMs have become the starting point for generalist robot policies because their web-scale pre-training transfers to open-world scenes and instructions \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,driess2023palm}. Yet VLM pre-training is dominated by high-level tasks such as visual question answering, so these models under-represent depth, object size, relative pose and physical dynamics \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,balazadeh2024synthetic,ghaffari2024exploring}. Precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes depend on exactly these cues.

A second family of policies pre-trains on predicting future frames or videos, which injects dynamics and spatial detail \citep{wu2023unleashing,du2024learning,black2023zero}. These models are not initialized from semantically rich VLMs, and tend to be weaker at semantic generalization. Figure~\ref{fig:teaser} summarizes this dichotomy, and Figure~\ref{fig:compare} contrasts the model types.

