We propose UP-VLA, which unifies both in one autoregressive backbone. The same language model is trained on MMU and on future image-token prediction, and at action time it sees a self-generated scene description together with the current visual tokens, so actions can use semantic and predictive information. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in a single autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention-masking scheme that mixes continuous MMU image tokens and discrete predictive tokens in one LLM (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism that conditions actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art CALVIN results (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, about 33\% over GR-1) and real-world gains in semantic generalization and precision tasks, with ablations in Section~\ref{sec:exp}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\textbf{Language-conditioned robot learning.} Early work maps instructions and observations to actions with imitation or reinforcement learning \citep{mei2016listen,misra2017mapping,lynch2020grounding,stepputtis2020language}, and large-scale imitation systems such as BC-Z and RT-1 scale demonstrations and task coverage \citep{jang2022bc,brohan2022rt}; CLIPort pairs pre-trained vision-language features with manipulation \citep{shridhar2022cliport}. Such policies are trained mostly on robot data. UP-VLA instead inherits an LLM backbone and keeps image-text co-training during action learning.

Language also enters through pre-trained representations and rewards, as in R3M and LIV \citep{nair2022r3m,ma2023liv}, and crowd-sourced language annotations on offline data \citep{nair2022learning}; surveys summarize language-informed reinforcement learning and robot language use \citep{luketina2019survey,tellex2020robots}. These works use language to specify tasks, whereas UP-VLA also uses language generation (scene description) as an intermediate signal for acting.

\textbf{LLMs and VLMs as planners and policies.} Another line uses language models to plan or write programs over robot skills \citep{ahn2022can,huang2022inner,liang2023code,huang2023voxposer}, and embodied multimodal models such as PaLM-E fold sensor input into the LLM \citep{driess2023palm}. Vision-language-action models fine-tune a VLM to emit actions: RT-2 transfers web knowledge to control \citep{brohan2023rt}, OpenVLA provides an open-source model \citep{kim24openvla}, and others add hierarchy, visual traces, or flow-based action heads \citep{belkhale2024rt,zheng2024tracevla,black2024pi_0}; Robo-Flamingo adapts a VLM into an imitator \citep{li2023vision} and 3D-VLA uses a 3D generative world model \citep{zhen20243d}. Further planners ground LLM plans in 3D scene graphs, simulated feasibility or situated programs \citep{rana2023sayplan,lin2023text2motion,singh2023progprompt}, and a survey reviews foundation models for general-purpose robots \citep{hu2023toward}. Open-source and dual-system VLAs continue this line \citep{cui2025openhelix,zhang2024hirt,song2025accelerating}, and the Open X-Embodiment collection supplies cross-robot data for such models \citep{o2023open}. These methods gain semantics but inherit the VLM's weak low-level and dynamics cues. UP-VLA adds an explicit future-prediction objective to the VLM-style backbone.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} Video and image generation supply dynamics priors: GR-1 pre-trains on large-scale video generation for manipulation \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE edits images into subgoals with diffusion \citep{black2023zero}, Uni-Pi generates text-guided video plans \citep{du2024learning}, and joint denoising predicts frames and actions together \citep{guo2024prediction}. These models are not initialized from semantic VLMs. Other work learns latent image-goal representations or goal-expressive video generation for control \citep{chen2024igor,zhang2025gevrm}, and large video generators such as Stable Video Diffusion and VideoPoet, or masked video transformers, demonstrate generative video modeling at scale \citep{blattmann2023stable,kondratyuk2023videopoet,yu2023magvit}. Diffusion models themselves rest on denoising probabilistic modeling \citep{ho2020denoising}. UP-VLA uses discrete VQ-GAN tokens \citep{esser2021taming} for prediction inside an LLM, keeping the semantic side through MMU.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Recent multimodal models unify comprehension and generation in one transformer \citep{xie2024show,ge2024seed,dong2023dreamllm}, and autoregressive LLMs can generate images \citep{sun2024autoregressive}. LM4LV shows that a frozen LLM can handle low-level vision tasks \citep{zheng2024lm4lv}, which is relevant to our aim of giving an LLM low-level visual competence. Work on spatial and physical reasoning in VLMs documents the gaps we target \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,wen2024can,li2024multimodal}. We bring this idea to embodied control, where the generation target is the future observation and the output also includes actions.

\section{Preliminaries}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given an observation $O_t$ and instruction $L$, the policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks} as the backbone. Two visual pathways are used: a CLIP-ViT \citep{radford2021learning} with an MLP projection $E_1$ produces continuous image tokens $u$ in the language embedding space (as in visual instruction tuning, \citealp{liu2024visual}), and a VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} produces discrete codebook tokens $v$, with a VQ decoder reconstructing images. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

Notation. $O_t$ is the observation at step $t$ and $O'_t$ an additional visual input; $\Delta t$ is the prediction horizon. $l$ denotes language tokens, $u$ continuous MMU image tokens from $E_1$, and $v_t$, $v'_j$ discrete tokens of the current and future image, with $M$ tokens per image. $\pi_\theta$ is the single LLM, with superscripts MMU and PRE marking the prompt format in use. Pre-trained VLMs of this kind are known to transfer to robots \citep{brohan2023rt}, but they are trained on understanding data only, which is why a prediction objective is added below.

\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA. (a) Pre-training mixes MMU on internet vision-language data (language understanding loss) with future prediction on video data (future prediction loss). (b) Fine-tuning first generates a scene description, then copies it into the extended instruction and jointly predicts future image tokens and actions.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:overview} gives the overview. UP-VLA is trained in two stages: pre-training on MMU data (LLaVA-665k) and on future prediction (25k Bridge demonstrations \citep{walke2023bridgedata}), then action fine-tuning on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompts, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantics.

\textbf{Unified prompting and attention masks.} Figure~\ref{fig:prompt} shows the prompts. For MMU the input is \texttt{[MMU]}, image tokens $u$, language tokens $l$; image tokens precede text so they attend to each other, and the model predicts next language tokens, $\hat H^{L}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. For prediction (PRE) the input is \texttt{[PRE]}, language $l$, current image tokens $v_t$; image tokens follow the text so they attend to all earlier tokens, and future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as next-token continuation, $\hat H^{O_{t+\Delta t}}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism for MMU and future prediction.}
\label{fig:prompt}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Joint prediction and understanding for action.} Given $(O_t,L)$, the model first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ for a prompt such as ``describe this image''. With additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$ embedded into language space, the extended instruction is $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. The model then predicts $(\hat H^{O_{t+\Delta t}},\hat H^{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$. A lightweight policy head (single-layer attention pooling, MAP, plus an MLP) maps action tokens to continuous actions, $\hat a_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat H^{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. Actions therefore attend to both the semantic description and the predictive visual tokens. At test time the same steps run in a loop: describe, form $L'$, predict, decode actions and execute.

\textbf{Why each component exists.} The two image pathways serve different purposes: continuous CLIP features carry semantics learned from image-text data, while discrete VQ-GAN tokens give a target space on which cross-entropy prediction of future frames is well defined and decodable back to images. Placing image tokens before text in MMU lets them attend to each other, as in standard visual instruction tuning, whereas in PRE placing them after text lets each image token condition on the instruction and on earlier image tokens, and the output positions are aligned with the future image rather than shifted by one. The scene description $s$ turns MMU output into an explicit semantic condition for actions, and the MAP head keeps action decoding light so that most capacity stays in the shared backbone. Because the same weights serve all three roles, understanding data can regularize the action model during fine-tuning, which is why MMU co-training continues in stage two.

\textbf{Objectives.} The MMU loss is the cross-entropy over next text tokens, $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{MMU}}=-\sum_i\log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$. The prediction loss is the cross-entropy over the $M$ future image tokens, $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{PRE}}=-\sum_{j=1}^{M}\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$. The action loss combines squared error on the position and binary cross-entropy on the gripper end state, $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\|\hat a_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\|_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$. The total is $\mathcal L=\lambda_1\mathcal L_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal L_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ACT}}$; the weights $\lambda_i$ are not reported in the material available to us.

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:exp}
\textbf{Setup.} In simulation we use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings: the agent must complete five chained tasks, and we report the success rate for $k$ tasks in a row and the average completed length. In the real world we use a Franka Emika Panda arm with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, and evaluate seen tasks, unseen-object grasping and precise operations (e.g., cable routing, small-object grasping), with 20 trials per task; Figure~\ref{fig:env} shows the environments. Baselines are VLA-based (Robo-Flamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2, an RT-2-style variant) and prediction-based (GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi, 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d}, Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}), plus RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt}. UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like model on our backbone without MMU. Pre-training uses LLaVA-tuning-665k for MMU and 25k Bridge demonstrations for prediction; downstream action data are the CALVIN demonstrations or our collected real-robot demonstrations. The CALVIN protocol is zero-shot on the held-out environment D in both settings: ABC$\rightarrow$D trains on scenes A, B, C, which requires generalizing to a new scene, while ABCD$\rightarrow$D trains on all four. Seen real-world tasks use the training skills, unseen-object tasks grasp objects absent from the demonstrations, and precise tasks need fine spatial accuracy. Entries marked * were reproduced by us; other baseline numbers are copied from the original papers.

