\section{Limitations}
Predicted images show background color shifts across scenes, which suggests insufficient visual-generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU sometimes misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale, so larger VLMs and richer data could reduce errors. Joint tasks add sequence length and training cost, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory could help. UP-VLA uses 2D images only; 3D inputs, multiple views or explicit physics modeling might further improve spatial understanding. Our comparisons also mix reproduced and copied baseline numbers, and no variance estimates are reported.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on both a scene description and predicted futures. It reaches 4.08 average length on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D, and improves real-world unseen-object and precise tasks, with ablations indicating complementary roles for MMU and prediction. The evidence is limited to CALVIN and one real-robot setup without variance estimates, and generative artifacts and scale limits remain open.

